WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.838 --> 00:00:04.333
ISA-95 is the international standard that

2
00:00:04.356 --> 00:00:07.073
describes how a plant’s systems fit together.

3
00:00:07.794 --> 00:00:10.406
Its first part appeared in 2000.

4
00:00:11.461 --> 00:00:13.992
Its levels give you one language with IT,

5
00:00:14.085 --> 00:00:18.624
with OT, the technology that controls machines, and with suppliers.

6
00:00:19.375 --> 00:00:22.603
Five, from zero to four: the floors of this plant.

7
00:00:24.045 --> 00:00:28.805
Level zero is the process itself: the plastic that becomes a cap in the mould.

8
00:00:29.881 --> 00:00:33.875
Level one measures and moves: sensors such as the temperature sensor,

9
00:00:34.061 --> 00:00:36.441
actuators such as the cooling-water valve.

10
00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:40.708
Level two monitors and controls: the PLC,

11
00:00:40.976 --> 00:00:43.588
the industrial computer that runs the machine.

12
00:00:44.301 --> 00:00:46.333
Some drawings put it at level one.

13
00:00:47.352 --> 00:00:49.326
SCADA belongs here too: with it,

14
00:00:49.477 --> 00:00:53.123
operators watch and run the whole installation on screens.

15
00:00:54.492 --> 00:00:59.891
Level three runs production, usually with an MES: which order on which machine,

16
00:01:00.053 --> 00:01:02.177
what was used, made and scrapped.

17
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:07.238
The quality system for inspection reports works per order and shift:

18
00:01:07.388 --> 00:01:08.515
level three too.

19
00:01:09.543 --> 00:01:14.396
Level four is business planning in the ERP, such as SAP: customer orders,

20
00:01:14.547 --> 00:01:16.393
purchasing, stock and cost.

21
00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:21.112
The standard is mostly about the boundary between three and four:

22
00:01:21.216 --> 00:01:23.968
which information goes back and forth there.

23
00:01:25.256 --> 00:01:27.915
The most useful distinction is the timescale:

24
00:01:28.008 --> 00:01:30.434
how fast decisions are made at a level.

25
00:01:31.469 --> 00:01:35.973
Level four thinks in months to days, level three in days to seconds,

26
00:01:36.171 --> 00:01:41.152
levels one and two in seconds and faster; the sensor even in milliseconds.

27
00:01:42.175 --> 00:01:43.452
A system down for an hour?

28
00:01:44.188 --> 00:01:49.470
Without the PLC the machine stops; without the ERP the line usually runs on.

29
00:01:50.474 --> 00:01:53.597
Typical mistake: we take OEE from SAP.

30
00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:58.541
The ERP knows what was booked, not what the machine did each minute.

31
00:01:59.291 --> 00:02:03.401
So ask: at which level does this number arise, and where do we use it?

32
00:02:04.763 --> 00:02:05.796
Follow one order.

33
00:02:06.547 --> 00:02:12.317
The ERP creates a production order: 2,000 caps of type A, ready on Thursday.

34
00:02:13.268 --> 00:02:16.542
The bill of materials lists the materials and how much:

35
00:02:16.635 --> 00:02:19.282
25 grams of granulate per cap.

36
00:02:20.017 --> 00:02:23.976
2,000 times 25 grams is 50 kilos.

37
00:02:24.992 --> 00:02:27.964
The routing says which operations, in which order,

38
00:02:28.057 --> 00:02:32.852
where and how long: injection moulding on machine 4, 12 seconds per cycle.

39
00:02:34.143 --> 00:02:39.182
Bill of materials and routing are master data: fixed information for many orders,

40
00:02:39.332 --> 00:02:42.769
kept in the ERP, with a copy in the MES.

41
00:02:43.795 --> 00:02:49.078
If it says 10 seconds while the machine manages 12, every shift looks too slow.

42
00:02:49.767 --> 00:02:54.574
Often master data were copied once at MES go-live and never kept up.

43
00:02:55.321 --> 00:02:58.282
So ask who changes a cycle time, and where.

44
00:02:59.274 --> 00:03:04.382
The MES spreads the work over lines and shifts: this order on Tuesday,

45
00:03:04.533 --> 00:03:06.356
early shift, machine 4.

46
00:03:07.361 --> 00:03:11.668
The operator starts it on the HMI, the touch screen on the machine.

47
00:03:12.423 --> 00:03:15.012
The PLC loads the settings for type A.

48
00:03:16.294 --> 00:03:19.904
The PLC counts every cycle, SCADA shows the progress,

49
00:03:20.137 --> 00:03:22.180
the historian stores the temperatures.

50
00:03:23.188 --> 00:03:30.491
At the end the MES reports 1,960 good and 40 scrap back to the ERP.

51
00:03:31.182 --> 00:03:33.331
Orders go down, data comes up.

52
00:03:34.376 --> 00:03:36.350
Usually the ERP has the order,

53
00:03:36.501 --> 00:03:40.565
bill of materials and stock; the MES the actual times,

54
00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:45.231
scrap with reasons and batches; the PLC the settings and counter.

55
00:03:46.224 --> 00:03:51.228
So that is in SAP and that is in the machine can both be true about the same number.

56
00:03:52.549 --> 00:03:57.019
Network security people draw almost the same layers: the Purdue model,

57
00:03:57.112 --> 00:04:01.744
from the early nineties, now mainly a network map: which system sits where,

58
00:04:01.907 --> 00:04:03.637
and which traffic may pass.

59
00:04:04.643 --> 00:04:06.849
ISA-95 built on it,

60
00:04:06.941 --> 00:04:08.590
but it is a functional model:

61
00:04:08.706 --> 00:04:12.712
which tasks exist and which information passes between them.

62
00:04:13.394 --> 00:04:16.192
It says nothing about cables or firewalls.

63
00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:23.418
Such a fixed exchange is an information flow, like the schedule from ERP to MES.

64
00:04:24.457 --> 00:04:28.660
In Purdue, zero to three are the plant, four the site’s office network,

65
00:04:28.846 --> 00:04:33.327
and five the enterprise network, with the central systems and the internet.

66
00:04:34.685 --> 00:04:37.274
Between three and four, at three point five,

67
00:04:37.483 --> 00:04:41.454
sits the industrial DMZ: a buffer network, added later.

68
00:04:42.164 --> 00:04:45.381
Office and plant both talk to it, not to each other.

69
00:04:46.463 --> 00:04:49.574
Level three is a task to an MES supplier,

70
00:04:49.714 --> 00:04:52.988
a place below the DMZ to a network administrator.

71
00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:56.530
So ask: what the system does, or where it sits?

72
00:04:57.228 --> 00:04:59.284
The numbering is a habit, not a law.

73
00:05:00.315 --> 00:05:05.667
A cloud MES that sends orders and gets counts back is functionally level three,

74
00:05:05.806 --> 00:05:07.652
but sits beyond level five.

75
00:05:08.348 --> 00:05:09.683
Does Purdue still fit?

76
00:05:10.501 --> 00:05:15.680
Most experts say yes, as a way of thinking, with new traffic through the DMZ.

77
00:05:16.746 --> 00:05:19.869
No connection started outside may reach the plant:

78
00:05:20.055 --> 00:05:22.784
a connector at level three calls out itself,

79
00:05:22.981 --> 00:05:27.718
to a relay in the DMZ that talks to the cloud; orders come in over that link.

80
00:05:28.756 --> 00:05:32.216
The security standard speaks of zones and conduits:

81
00:05:32.309 --> 00:05:35.850
groups of devices and the controlled connections between them.

82
00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:39.468
Record both maps, and behind neat layers,

83
00:05:39.666 --> 00:05:43.718
ask for the firewall rules: often everything hangs in one network.
